00:03:54.810 --> 00:03:58.050
is just about two
micrograms of DNA,

00:03:58.050 --> 00:04:00.320
if you try to sequence that DNA.

00:04:00.320 --> 00:04:03.690
Or from Google's standpoint,
a petabyte of data

00:04:03.690 --> 00:04:06.970
is how much Google
gets every day.

00:04:06.970 --> 00:04:09.750
That's the amount of video
that Google gets uploaded

00:04:09.750 --> 00:04:12.960
into YouTube on a daily basis.

00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:15.360
And at this point, you
can say, look, Carl,

00:04:15.360 --> 00:04:18.300
I don't do genomics, or
my company is not Google.

00:04:18.300 --> 00:04:20.337
So it's not relevant to us.

00:04:20.337 --> 00:04:22.170
But I challenge you to
think about petabytes

00:04:22.170 --> 00:04:24.070
of data in the following way.

00:04:24.070 --> 00:04:27.630
So think about application
servers or web servers.

00:04:27.630 --> 00:04:29.430
Many companies, many
projects out there

00:04:29.430 --> 00:04:32.970
have hundreds of these servers.

00:04:32.970 --> 00:04:35.520
200 servers is not
a large number.

00:04:35.520 --> 00:04:38.070
And if you think about
these application servers,

00:04:38.070 --> 00:04:42.330
just generating log data, just
logging maybe website visitors

00:04:42.330 --> 00:04:46.540
at 50 entries a second, if you
don't have a petabyte of data

00:04:46.540 --> 00:04:50.900
now, in three years, with
these 200 application servers,

00:04:50.900 --> 00:04:53.520
you will have a petabyte
of data to analyze.

00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:56.230
So the point is, even if
a vast majority of you

00:04:56.230 --> 00:05:00.690
are not analyzing petabytes
of data today, you will soon.

00:05:00.690 --> 00:05:03.060
So what is that data?

00:05:03.060 --> 00:05:07.200
And I'm sure many of you know
that the studies have shown

00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:11.190
that 80% to 90% of that
data, the data in the world,

00:05:11.190 --> 00:05:13.170
is unstructured data.

00:05:13.170 --> 00:05:15.510
But I think if you here
at the BigQuery session,

00:05:15.510 --> 00:05:18.630
you've probably have had some
experience with SQL, with using

00:05:18.630 --> 00:05:23.010
BigQuery, and you're comfortable
with structured data, the data

00:05:23.010 --> 00:05:25.830
that's organized into
rows and columns, data

00:05:25.830 --> 00:05:27.150
with a relational schema.

00:05:27.150 --> 00:05:29.050
And that's what you
want to analyze.

00:05:29.050 --> 00:05:30.810
So it seems like a
challenge to think

00:05:30.810 --> 00:05:34.500
about all this unstructured
data, all these images,

00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:38.280
natural language text, videos,
and think about how you

00:05:38.280 --> 00:05:40.500
can get value out of that data.

00:05:40.500 --> 00:05:44.530
Well, yes, it's
impossible, really,

00:05:44.530 --> 00:05:47.340
to analyze unstructured
data using SQL.

00:05:47.340 --> 00:05:49.590
But there's another very
important category of data

00:05:49.590 --> 00:05:51.810
that I want to draw
your attention to.

00:05:51.810 --> 00:05:53.866
And that's semi-structured data.

00:05:53.866 --> 00:05:55.990
So if you want to think
about semi-structured data,

00:05:55.990 --> 00:05:59.190
think about data in
a format like JSON,

00:05:59.190 --> 00:06:03.480
or XML, or even some
key value databases.

00:06:03.480 --> 00:06:07.200
It's the data where you may
have some relational schema.

00:06:07.200 --> 00:06:10.050
You can think about
parts of JSON objects

00:06:10.050 --> 00:06:15.720
in terms of the schema that has
strings, or integers, or dates.

00:06:15.720 --> 00:06:17.580
But at the same time,
it has some data

00:06:17.580 --> 00:06:21.240
that doesn't really fit into
relational, traditional rows

00:06:21.240 --> 00:06:22.710
and columns formats.

00:06:22.710 --> 00:06:25.950
Think arrays, or
think about key value

00:06:25.950 --> 00:06:30.150
pairs, of dictionaries in JSON.

00:06:30.150 --> 00:06:33.900
So it seems like there's
an interesting category

00:06:33.900 --> 00:06:36.210
in the middle between
unstructured and structured

00:06:36.210 --> 00:06:37.360
data types.

00:06:37.360 --> 00:06:40.650
So it seems like it might be
interesting in some cases.

00:06:40.650 --> 00:06:44.070
Are there any practical
examples of this kind of data?

00:06:44.070 --> 00:06:47.130
Well, I just wanted to mention
this recent announcement.

00:06:47.130 --> 00:06:50.270
This was announced back
in May of this year.

00:06:50.270 --> 00:06:53.290
Twitter, the social
networking company,

00:06:53.290 --> 00:06:56.650
actually decided to migrate
their infrastructure over

00:06:56.650 --> 00:06:58.260
to Google Cloud.

00:06:58.260 --> 00:07:01.540
And think about the
contents of these tweets.

00:07:01.540 --> 00:07:04.720
This is a perfect example
of semi-structured data.

00:07:04.720 --> 00:07:07.000
Just look at the
tweet right here.

00:07:07.000 --> 00:07:08.920
It has natural language text.

00:07:08.920 --> 00:07:10.840
But at the same time,
this kind of data

00:07:10.840 --> 00:07:14.100
contains embedded at mentions.

00:07:14.100 --> 00:07:16.480
This contains hyperlinks.

00:07:16.480 --> 00:07:18.320
And it also has some
structured information

00:07:18.320 --> 00:07:21.000
with pointers to graphic.

00:07:21.000 --> 00:07:24.100
It has dates, et cetera.

00:07:24.100 --> 00:07:26.580
So this is a perfect
example of a kind

00:07:26.580 --> 00:07:29.280
of data that exists in
a semi-structured format

00:07:29.280 --> 00:07:31.770
that many companies
may want to analyze.

00:07:31.770 --> 00:07:34.260
And now that Twitter is
moving to Google Cloud,

00:07:34.260 --> 00:07:36.630
they'd be able to
use Google Cloud

00:07:36.630 --> 00:07:40.800
capabilities like BigQuery
to analyze this type of data.

00:07:40.800 --> 00:07:43.140
And if you're still
skeptical, I just

00:07:43.140 --> 00:07:46.770
wanted to give you a few
more concrete examples.

00:07:46.770 --> 00:07:49.230
In preparing for this
talk, I actually went out

00:07:49.230 --> 00:07:51.420
and thought about the
developer activity

00:07:51.420 --> 00:07:53.105
around semi-structured data.

00:07:53.105 --> 00:07:54.480
So the first place
that I went to

00:07:54.480 --> 00:07:57.540
was GitHub, the source
code repository.

00:07:57.540 --> 00:08:01.860
And I went out and tried to
analyze the GitHub commits

00:08:01.860 --> 00:08:05.970
or file changes to various
structured and semi-structured

00:08:05.970 --> 00:08:07.390
data formats.

00:08:07.390 --> 00:08:08.740
So this is what I came up with.

00:08:08.740 --> 00:08:11.760
I actually looked at
just the top five formats

00:08:11.760 --> 00:08:13.500
that you see listed
on the slide.

00:08:13.500 --> 00:08:15.300
And if you're
wondering why top five,

00:08:15.300 --> 00:08:19.140
it's because other
formats like DDLs for SQL

00:08:19.140 --> 00:08:22.860
and others actually
resulted in too few

00:08:22.860 --> 00:08:28.480
commits to make a significant
bar on this chart.

00:08:28.480 --> 00:08:30.240
So this is one
interesting data point.

00:08:30.240 --> 00:08:34.289
You can see over 100
million commits for JSON

00:08:34.289 --> 00:08:38.190
relative to about 10 million
for CSV, which is commonly

00:08:38.190 --> 00:08:40.650
used for structured data.

00:08:40.650 --> 00:08:43.140
And at this point, you
could say, so what?

00:08:43.140 --> 00:08:45.390
All right, so this
is GitHub commits.

00:08:45.390 --> 00:08:49.650
On one hand, GitHub is not
really a data repository.

00:08:49.650 --> 00:08:51.390
And in the same time,
developers often

00:08:51.390 --> 00:08:55.260
use JSON and XML as their
configuration files, right,

00:08:55.260 --> 00:08:57.270
something that they would
change all the time.

00:08:57.270 --> 00:08:58.260
And that's fair.

00:08:58.260 --> 00:09:01.500
So I went out and looked at
other sites for developers.

00:09:01.500 --> 00:09:04.080
The first one I looked
at was Stack Overflow.

00:09:04.080 --> 00:09:05.730
And you'll notice
a pattern, right?

00:09:05.730 --> 00:09:09.000
So the similar type
of proportion exists.

00:09:09.000 --> 00:09:10.710
If you look at the
semi-structured data

00:09:10.710 --> 00:09:13.830
formats like JSON
and XML, they receive

00:09:13.830 --> 00:09:17.250
significantly more
questions than CSV

00:09:17.250 --> 00:09:18.680
and various variants of SQL.

00:09:18.680 --> 00:09:21.300
So in this case, I actually
looked at the questions

00:09:21.300 --> 00:09:23.429
for DDL formats as well.

00:09:23.429 --> 00:09:24.720
So that's just two data points.

00:09:24.720 --> 00:09:28.660
But if you go and look
at other data sources,

00:09:28.660 --> 00:09:34.050
like the very well-known Hacker
News, social media website,

00:09:34.050 --> 00:09:35.490
a similar pattern exists.

00:09:35.490 --> 00:09:38.040
In fact, I went out and
looked at Google Trends,

00:09:38.040 --> 00:09:40.290
Google's own data repository.

00:09:40.290 --> 00:09:42.900
And there's roughly
the same proportion.

00:09:42.900 --> 00:09:45.750
If you compare the
developer activity

00:09:45.750 --> 00:09:49.320
around structured and
semi-structured data formats,

00:09:49.320 --> 00:09:51.780
about 80% to 90%
of that activity

00:09:51.780 --> 00:09:55.680
happens around JSON, XML,
YAML, the semi-structured data

00:09:55.680 --> 00:09:58.380
formats, as opposed to
structured data formats

00:09:58.380 --> 00:10:01.930
that I lumped here
together with CSV, TSV,

00:10:01.930 --> 00:10:06.540
tab-separated formats, SQL,
DDL, and even Parquet and Avro.

00:10:06.540 --> 00:10:09.420
So the point is, there
is something there.

00:10:09.420 --> 00:10:13.080
There's a valuable set of data
that exists that developers

00:10:13.080 --> 00:10:14.220
are working with.

00:10:14.220 --> 00:10:15.840
But you need to ask
yourself, are you

00:10:15.840 --> 00:10:18.900
getting insights
out of this data?

00:10:18.900 --> 00:10:20.190
Clearly it's important.

00:10:20.190 --> 00:10:21.750
This amount of data is growing.

00:10:21.750 --> 00:10:23.330
But what can you do about it?

00:10:23.330 --> 00:10:25.080
It seems like, since
it's semi-structured,

00:10:25.080 --> 00:10:27.750
traditional SQL is not
really the right tool.

00:10:27.750 --> 00:10:29.910
And this is what I want to--

00:10:29.910 --> 00:10:32.250
that's the point
where I want to change

00:10:32.250 --> 00:10:34.110
your opinion in this session.

00:10:34.110 --> 00:10:39.540
So how many of you are
first timers to BigQuery?

00:10:39.540 --> 00:10:42.950
How many of you have never
heard of BigQuery before today?

00:10:42.950 --> 00:10:43.530
Oh, my god.

00:10:43.530 --> 00:10:46.740
This is the first time I
think I am seeing single digit

00:10:46.740 --> 00:10:49.290
hands going up in this room.

00:10:49.290 --> 00:10:51.390
So it looks like most
of you know BigQuery.

00:10:51.390 --> 00:10:54.480
You know it's a
petabyte-scale data warehouse

00:10:54.480 --> 00:10:56.610
available from Google Cloud.

00:10:56.610 --> 00:10:58.830
It can get you
results in seconds.

00:10:58.830 --> 00:11:01.030
And some of the reasons
why it's so fast

00:11:01.030 --> 00:11:04.170
is using this columnar-based
storage format

00:11:04.170 --> 00:11:07.560
that, behind the scenes, is
supported by a file system that

00:11:07.560 --> 00:11:11.880
automatically shards the data
and makes it available to you

00:11:11.880 --> 00:11:15.450
as a BigQuery user at
extremely high throughput rates

00:11:15.450 --> 00:11:17.550
with very low latency.

00:11:17.550 --> 00:11:19.650
And what you'll
look at in a moment

00:11:19.650 --> 00:11:23.880
is the support for two types
of data structures in BigQuery

00:11:23.880 --> 00:11:26.460
known as arrays and struts.

00:11:26.460 --> 00:11:29.460
And what I'll show you is
that both of these data types

00:11:29.460 --> 00:11:32.200
are part of SQL 2011 standard.

00:11:32.200 --> 00:11:35.650
So if you are already
a SQL guru but have not

00:11:35.650 --> 00:11:38.970
got a chance to work with these
two types of data structures,

00:11:38.970 --> 00:11:41.730
I think you will find a
lot of interesting examples

00:11:41.730 --> 00:11:43.050
in this session.

00:11:43.050 --> 00:11:45.510
So one thing you need
to know about BigQuery

00:11:45.510 --> 00:11:47.940
is that it consists
of two parts.

00:11:47.940 --> 00:11:51.010
There's BigQuery storage,
that is the data warehouse

00:11:51.010 --> 00:11:54.130
piece that maintains,
warehouses your data,

00:11:54.130 --> 00:11:56.970
and then there's the
BigQuery analysis engine

00:11:56.970 --> 00:12:00.480
that takes your SQL
statements and uses those SQL

00:12:00.480 --> 00:12:02.670
statements to analyze data.

00:12:02.670 --> 00:12:04.950
And what's great about
BigQuery is that, first of all,

00:12:04.950 --> 00:12:09.870
it supports various types of
data formats and data input

00:12:09.870 --> 00:12:10.900
sources.

00:12:10.900 --> 00:12:12.930
For example, you can
get data into BigQuery

00:12:12.930 --> 00:12:15.810
from something like Google
Analytics, Firebase,

00:12:15.810 --> 00:12:19.650
other services on Google
Cloud, like Cloud Datastore,

00:12:19.650 --> 00:12:23.910
and have that data reside
inside of BigQuery storage.

00:12:23.910 --> 00:12:26.340
And another thing that
is great about BigQuery

00:12:26.340 --> 00:12:31.350
is that it allows you to use the
BigQuery analysis engine to run

00:12:31.350 --> 00:12:34.110
SQL statements against
data that resides

00:12:34.110 --> 00:12:36.240
outside of BigQuery storage.

00:12:36.240 --> 00:12:37.830
So for example, you
can use BigQuery

00:12:37.830 --> 00:12:41.700
to analyze data in something
like Cloud Storage buckets

00:12:41.700 --> 00:12:44.730
or in Cloud Bigtable.

00:12:44.730 --> 00:12:48.990
And BigQuery is used by
customers throughout the world.

00:12:48.990 --> 00:12:51.660
In fact, at this point,
BigQuery supports

00:12:51.660 --> 00:12:57.130
exabytes of data for
Google Cloud customers.

00:12:57.130 --> 00:13:00.970
Now, let's wrap up with
the introductory part

00:13:00.970 --> 00:13:04.300
of the presentation and shift
into something more technical.

00:13:04.300 --> 00:13:06.550
So what I'd like to
do next is show you

00:13:06.550 --> 00:13:09.370
an example of analyzing
semi-structured data

00:13:09.370 --> 00:13:10.960
with BigQuery.

00:13:10.960 --> 00:13:13.240
And here's what I'll show you.

00:13:13.240 --> 00:13:17.290
Suppose I want to build an
analysis engine to find out,

00:13:17.290 --> 00:13:19.420
what do developers care about?

00:13:19.420 --> 00:13:21.740
Developers are early adopters.

00:13:21.740 --> 00:13:23.536
They sort of foresee trends.

00:13:23.536 --> 00:13:24.910
So what I'd like
to be able to do

00:13:24.910 --> 00:13:27.400
is get data from
the website known

00:13:27.400 --> 00:13:29.920
as Hacker News, the
social media website,

00:13:29.920 --> 00:13:32.170
figure out what a
developer is talking about,

00:13:32.170 --> 00:13:33.790
and then use that
information to help

00:13:33.790 --> 00:13:39.190
me guide my decisions about
which technologies to learn.

00:13:39.190 --> 00:13:41.080
And before this
session, I actually

00:13:41.080 --> 00:13:44.530
went out to Hacker News,
and I used their APIs

00:13:44.530 --> 00:13:47.890
that happened to be JSON-based
APIs, JavaScript Object

00:13:47.890 --> 00:13:52.180
Notation-based APIs, to download
some sample data on my laptop.

00:13:52.180 --> 00:13:55.870
And what I'll do next is
import that data into BigQuery

00:13:55.870 --> 00:13:57.160
here on stage.

00:13:57.160 --> 00:13:59.950
Now, the way that
I'll import this data

00:13:59.950 --> 00:14:01.780
is that I'll take
this route shown

00:14:01.780 --> 00:14:03.880
on the screen with letter A.

00:14:03.880 --> 00:14:06.820
So imagine I have my JSON file.

00:14:06.820 --> 00:14:09.730
And I'll use the web
UI that you see here

00:14:09.730 --> 00:14:13.300
to import this data into
the BigQuery storage.

00:14:13.300 --> 00:14:15.415
Now, I can use the web UI.

00:14:15.415 --> 00:14:17.380
I can also use
command-line interface

00:14:17.380 --> 00:14:19.420
from BigQuery, known as bq tool.

00:14:19.420 --> 00:14:22.030
I can even use BigQuery
application programming

00:14:22.030 --> 00:14:24.340
interfaces, APIs, to do that.

00:14:24.340 --> 00:14:28.660
Now, that's the approach that
I'll take here during the demo.

00:14:28.660 --> 00:14:31.220
If you're planning to
import data into BigQuery

00:14:31.220 --> 00:14:33.340
in production, please--

00:14:33.340 --> 00:14:36.350
I really ask you, please--
don't use this approach.

00:14:36.350 --> 00:14:37.790
And here's why.

00:14:37.790 --> 00:14:40.600
If you're trying to
import data into BigQuery

00:14:40.600 --> 00:14:42.250
using this direct
route, there are

00:14:42.250 --> 00:14:44.170
many things that can go wrong.

00:14:44.170 --> 00:14:46.360
One of them, if you're
using a browser,

00:14:46.360 --> 00:14:48.760
browsers have limitations
on the sizes of files

00:14:48.760 --> 00:14:50.080
that you can upload.

00:14:50.080 --> 00:14:51.700
Browsers can crash.

00:14:51.700 --> 00:14:54.160
The network connections
can get interrupted.

00:14:54.160 --> 00:14:56.500
So if you're thinking
about importing data

00:14:56.500 --> 00:14:58.960
into BigQuery storage
and production, what

00:14:58.960 --> 00:15:01.390
I'd like you to do is
follow this route shown

00:15:01.390 --> 00:15:03.580
with letter B on the slide.

00:15:03.580 --> 00:15:05.480
So here's how it works.

00:15:05.480 --> 00:15:08.010
You're going to take a
tool known as gsutil.

00:15:08.010 --> 00:15:09.700
It's a command-line utility.

00:15:09.700 --> 00:15:13.120
You will take your data and
copy this data into something

00:15:13.120 --> 00:15:15.430
called Google Cloud Storage.

00:15:15.430 --> 00:15:17.800
And the great thing
about this gsutil tool

00:15:17.800 --> 00:15:20.800
is that it will be a
full-tolerant upload

00:15:20.800 --> 00:15:23.650
for your data, meaning
that gsutil will actually

00:15:23.650 --> 00:15:27.820
resume data transfers if there
is a failure in the network

00:15:27.820 --> 00:15:29.320
connections.

00:15:29.320 --> 00:15:31.270
Gsutil will actually
take checkpoints

00:15:31.270 --> 00:15:33.520
to make sure that it
doesn't have to re-upload

00:15:33.520 --> 00:15:34.660
the entire data set.

00:15:34.660 --> 00:15:38.200
It only re-uploads the changes
after the network connection.

00:15:38.200 --> 00:15:40.450
And one of the coolest
things about gsutil

00:15:40.450 --> 00:15:43.060
is that it supports
multithreaded uploading, which

00:15:43.060 --> 00:15:45.700
means that you can control
how much bandwidth you

00:15:45.700 --> 00:15:48.520
may want to allocate
to this gsutil tool.

00:15:48.520 --> 00:15:52.000
And then after you moved
your data via gsutil

00:15:52.000 --> 00:15:54.550
into Google Cloud
Storage, then your data

00:15:54.550 --> 00:15:56.260
is inside of Google
Cloud, and you

00:15:56.260 --> 00:15:58.750
can use the rest of these tools
that you see on the screen

00:15:58.750 --> 00:16:01.510
to get your data into BigQuery.

00:16:01.510 --> 00:16:03.490
All right, so that's
enough with an overview.

00:16:03.490 --> 00:16:05.680
Let's switch over to
the demo, and I'll

00:16:05.680 --> 00:16:08.470
show you how this works.

00:16:08.470 --> 00:16:11.860
So as you can see right now,
I'm looking at the BigQuery user

00:16:11.860 --> 00:16:12.640
interface.

00:16:12.640 --> 00:16:15.310
This should look very
familiar to most of you.

00:16:15.310 --> 00:16:18.790
And notice that here I
have my BigQuery project

00:16:18.790 --> 00:16:20.590
with this long name,
but we're going

00:16:20.590 --> 00:16:23.110
to call it a Qwiklabs project.

00:16:23.110 --> 00:16:25.480
So to import data
into this project,

00:16:25.480 --> 00:16:28.120
I need to create something
called a data set.

00:16:28.120 --> 00:16:31.570
A data set is simply a container
for the various data tables

00:16:31.570 --> 00:16:33.070
that I have inside of BigQuery.

00:16:33.070 --> 00:16:35.210
So I am uploading
Hacker News data.

00:16:35.210 --> 00:16:38.080
Let me just call it HN,
short for Hacker News.

00:16:38.080 --> 00:16:40.430
I'm not going to specify
the location for this data.

00:16:40.430 --> 00:16:41.800
I'll leave it unspecified.

00:16:41.800 --> 00:16:44.380
But if I wanted to, I
could have set up data

00:16:44.380 --> 00:16:48.970
residency for this data set
in the US, EU, or Tokyo.

00:16:48.970 --> 00:16:51.680
OK, so now I have
this new data set.

00:16:51.680 --> 00:16:53.560
Let's actually go
ahead and create

00:16:53.560 --> 00:16:56.050
a new table in this data set.

00:16:56.050 --> 00:16:59.370
So I'll click Create New Table.

00:16:59.370 --> 00:17:03.730
And after I close the editor
on the right-hand side,

00:17:03.730 --> 00:17:05.980
you'll notice that, on the
right side of my screen,

00:17:05.980 --> 00:17:09.510
I have this option to create
a table from source data.

00:17:09.510 --> 00:17:12.790
And as I pointed out moments
ago, just for this demo,

00:17:12.790 --> 00:17:16.829
I'll upload a file into this
table from my local file

00:17:16.829 --> 00:17:17.819
system.

00:17:17.819 --> 00:17:20.969
And this file is going
to be a JSON document.

00:17:20.969 --> 00:17:23.010
If you're wondering what
it looks like, remember,

00:17:23.010 --> 00:17:25.990
this is the data that I
received from the Hacker News

00:17:25.990 --> 00:17:28.349
website via the APIs.

00:17:28.349 --> 00:17:30.180
So I cleaned it up a little bit.

00:17:30.180 --> 00:17:31.770
And if you look
at the data here,

00:17:31.770 --> 00:17:34.330
you can see that it's
typical JSON object.

00:17:34.330 --> 00:17:39.210
So I have a date, and
then I have these stories.

00:17:39.210 --> 00:17:42.120
And now this starts looking
very different from most

00:17:42.120 --> 00:17:44.190
of relational tables out there.

00:17:44.190 --> 00:17:46.050
Notice that I have
a single date.

00:17:46.050 --> 00:17:49.830
And now within these stories,
I have array of values.

00:17:49.830 --> 00:17:52.236
These are titles of
articles, the number

00:17:52.236 --> 00:17:53.610
of times they have
been uploaded,

00:17:53.610 --> 00:17:57.640
the score, the URL to these
articles, and so forth.

00:17:57.640 --> 00:17:59.140
So this is what the
data looks like.

00:17:59.140 --> 00:18:02.490
Let's go ahead and try to
upload this into BigQuery.

00:18:02.490 --> 00:18:04.740
So I'm going to
do a file upload.

00:18:04.740 --> 00:18:07.820
I'm going to choose
my JSON document.

00:18:07.820 --> 00:18:12.290
And lets call this destination
table as HN for Hacker News.

00:18:12.290 --> 00:18:14.990
And since this is
from December 2008,

00:18:14.990 --> 00:18:17.750
I'll use 2008 as the suffix.

00:18:17.750 --> 00:18:20.750
Now, I could have specified
the schema manually.

00:18:20.750 --> 00:18:22.970
But here I'll trust
BigQuery to figure out

00:18:22.970 --> 00:18:26.520
the scheme of this JSON
document automatically.

00:18:26.520 --> 00:18:28.850
Let's go ahead and
click Create Table.

00:18:28.850 --> 00:18:30.790
And when I do this,
BigQuery creates

00:18:30.790 --> 00:18:32.600
something known as a job.

00:18:32.600 --> 00:18:34.310
So in this case, the
job happens to be

00:18:34.310 --> 00:18:37.770
to load the data inside
of the BigQuery table.

00:18:37.770 --> 00:18:39.540
I can see that the
job is complete.

00:18:39.540 --> 00:18:42.920
So let's actually take
a look at this table.

00:18:42.920 --> 00:18:45.380
Let's preview the
contents of this table.

00:18:45.380 --> 00:18:47.600
And this should look familiar.

00:18:47.600 --> 00:18:50.040
Notice that I have a
single row of data.

00:18:50.040 --> 00:18:53.120
Here's the URL to a
story from Hacker News.

00:18:53.120 --> 00:18:54.860
Here's the score,
the number of times

00:18:54.860 --> 00:18:57.620
that it has been
uploaded, its title,

00:18:57.620 --> 00:19:00.040
and then there's the date.

00:19:00.040 --> 00:19:03.560
Now, if you've used traditional
relational databases

00:19:03.560 --> 00:19:06.860
in the past, this should
start to look weird.

00:19:06.860 --> 00:19:08.630
What happened to this date?

00:19:08.630 --> 00:19:11.480
I have all these
values with the title,

00:19:11.480 --> 00:19:14.600
but now they're
blanks for the date.

00:19:14.600 --> 00:19:16.450
Where did the date go?

00:19:16.450 --> 00:19:20.440
And notice that I have many of
these titles for a single date.

00:19:20.440 --> 00:19:22.450
So how do I analyze this?

00:19:22.450 --> 00:19:23.880
Let's take a look at the schema.

00:19:23.880 --> 00:19:26.170
Maybe, yes, it has some hints.

00:19:26.170 --> 00:19:27.460
Well, this looks familiar.

00:19:27.460 --> 00:19:28.340
There's a date.

00:19:28.340 --> 00:19:31.020
It's nullable, so date
is a standard SQL type.

00:19:31.020 --> 00:19:32.890
There are strings, integers.

00:19:32.890 --> 00:19:34.480
All of that looks familiar.

00:19:34.480 --> 00:19:37.330
But now there are these stories.

00:19:37.330 --> 00:19:39.640
And if you've never
seen this before,

00:19:39.640 --> 00:19:43.270
records repeated,
what are these?

00:19:43.270 --> 00:19:44.740
So I've been doing
SQL for a while,

00:19:44.740 --> 00:19:46.660
but now I'm totally confused.

00:19:46.660 --> 00:19:48.090
So I don't know what to do.

00:19:48.090 --> 00:19:50.590
And at this point, I'd like to
introduce my colleague, Evan,

00:19:50.590 --> 00:19:51.550
back on stage.

00:19:51.550 --> 00:19:54.190
He is going to give us the
tools to better analyze

00:19:54.190 --> 00:19:56.961
this record-repeated
strangeness in the schema.

00:19:56.961 --> 00:19:58.002
EVAN JONES: Thanks, Carl.

00:20:03.460 --> 00:20:03.990
Cool.

00:20:03.990 --> 00:20:05.584
So whenever you
have any problems

00:20:05.584 --> 00:20:07.500
with nested and repeated
fields, just call me.

00:20:07.500 --> 00:20:09.750
That's the solution,
end of demo.

00:20:09.750 --> 00:20:10.949
No, I'm kidding.

00:20:10.949 --> 00:20:12.240
This, I'll say it blew me away.

00:20:12.240 --> 00:20:14.460
So I've been working on
a SQL for 10, 15 years.

00:20:14.460 --> 00:20:16.844
And before I was smart
enough, as Carl mentioned,

00:20:16.844 --> 00:20:18.510
to go into the schema
and look for that,

00:20:18.510 --> 00:20:19.593
I was looking at the data.

00:20:19.593 --> 00:20:21.900
And the first thing that,
exactly is Carl mentioned,

00:20:21.900 --> 00:20:23.440
you have one row.

00:20:23.440 --> 00:20:24.870
And for me being
a structured data

00:20:24.870 --> 00:20:27.060
guy in the background
as a data analyst,

00:20:27.060 --> 00:20:28.600
scrolling over and
seeing the date

00:20:28.600 --> 00:20:30.320
here and what I saw
it was null records,

00:20:30.320 --> 00:20:31.290
I kind of lost my mind.

00:20:31.290 --> 00:20:33.150
I'm like, oh, this is
really, really weird.

00:20:33.150 --> 00:20:36.520
Even still, you have what
you thought was one field,

00:20:36.520 --> 00:20:38.220
but it has a dot in it.

00:20:38.220 --> 00:20:39.570
You've got stories dot title.

00:20:39.570 --> 00:20:40.905
You've got stories dot score.

00:20:40.905 --> 00:20:43.280
So it almost looks like it's
two different tables in one.

00:20:43.280 --> 00:20:44.580
You've got dates, and
you've got articles,

00:20:44.580 --> 00:20:46.510
and you have the details
all in one place.

00:20:46.510 --> 00:20:48.180
So that's-- we're going to
get into the magic as we work

00:20:48.180 --> 00:20:49.590
through some of these SQL
queries that you're going

00:20:49.590 --> 00:20:50.230
to see.

00:20:50.230 --> 00:20:52.140
But the real exciting
part about it

00:20:52.140 --> 00:20:55.200
for me personally, even if
your data isn't in this format

00:20:55.200 --> 00:20:56.365
already, it could be.

00:20:56.365 --> 00:20:57.990
And that's the really,
really fun part,

00:20:57.990 --> 00:21:01.080
is because, in a traditional
relational database

00:21:01.080 --> 00:21:03.730
sense, normally you might
have multiple different tables

00:21:03.730 --> 00:21:06.510
stored in multiple different
places in a technical term

00:21:06.510 --> 00:21:08.700
that we called normalization.

00:21:08.700 --> 00:21:10.450
And that's great for
relational databases.

00:21:10.450 --> 00:21:12.033
But BigQuery doesn't
operate that way.

00:21:12.033 --> 00:21:14.670
And you get a huge, huge
potential performance boost

00:21:14.670 --> 00:21:16.590
by bringing your data
together, potentially

00:21:16.590 --> 00:21:20.414
in what I like to call a
pre-joined or a nested way.

00:21:20.414 --> 00:21:22.080
So let's run through
a bunch of queries.

00:21:22.080 --> 00:21:23.670
And I've got some cool
questions for you guys, too.

00:21:23.670 --> 00:21:26.169
Unfortunately, I wish I had
some swag or something like that

00:21:26.169 --> 00:21:28.790
to throw out in the crowd, but
you'll just have to make do.

00:21:28.790 --> 00:21:31.096
So first and foremost, if
you're familiar with SQL,

00:21:31.096 --> 00:21:32.720
you've probably seen
a lot select star.

00:21:32.720 --> 00:21:34.490
And looking at some of these
might be just a little bit

00:21:34.490 --> 00:21:35.900
baffling, but stick with it.

00:21:35.900 --> 00:21:38.066
So the first thing we want
to do is you can actually

00:21:38.066 --> 00:21:39.050
create array types.

00:21:39.050 --> 00:21:40.770
This is a nice, booming voice.

00:21:40.770 --> 00:21:43.354
You can create array
types directly into SQL.

00:21:43.354 --> 00:21:45.770
So what I'm going to do, I'm
still in the BigQuery web UI.

00:21:45.770 --> 00:21:50.030
If you didn't know it already,
if you actually highlight

00:21:50.030 --> 00:21:51.180
a portion of the query--

00:21:51.180 --> 00:21:54.530
I'm hitting
Command-E on the Mac.

00:21:54.530 --> 00:21:56.750
But you can actually execute
just a partial statement

00:21:56.750 --> 00:21:57.440
of the SQL.

00:21:57.440 --> 00:21:59.390
So I've got a long
SQL statement that I'm

00:21:59.390 --> 00:22:02.000
going to walk through
and execute each portion,

00:22:02.000 --> 00:22:02.900
as you see.

00:22:02.900 --> 00:22:04.835
So I've got an array
that I want to create.

00:22:04.835 --> 00:22:06.710
As you saw in the image
a little bit earlier,

00:22:06.710 --> 00:22:08.168
I have four different
fruits that I

00:22:08.168 --> 00:22:10.100
want to fill into my
little array basket.

00:22:10.100 --> 00:22:12.030
So I'm saying this is
going to be an array.

00:22:12.030 --> 00:22:14.690
An array, again,
is just going to be

00:22:14.690 --> 00:22:18.420
a collection of those items
that share a singular data type.

00:22:18.420 --> 00:22:20.420
So that's the critical
part, is that I'm saying,

00:22:20.420 --> 00:22:22.010
hey, everything that's going
to be in this collection

00:22:22.010 --> 00:22:23.490
is going to be a string.

00:22:23.490 --> 00:22:25.447
So arrays have to share
the single data type.

00:22:25.447 --> 00:22:27.530
That's going to come back
in one of the key points

00:22:27.530 --> 00:22:28.980
that we talk about later.

00:22:28.980 --> 00:22:31.809
So as you saw on the
results, again, if--

00:22:31.809 --> 00:22:34.100
I haven't really worked a
lot with arrays and BigQuery.

00:22:34.100 --> 00:22:35.487
This might look super strange.

00:22:35.487 --> 00:22:37.820
And the really cool part is,
if you hover over the type,

00:22:37.820 --> 00:22:39.920
you see, hey, not only
is it a string in there,

00:22:39.920 --> 00:22:41.097
but it's repeated.

00:22:41.097 --> 00:22:43.180
So that's how you get your
repeated data in there.

00:22:43.180 --> 00:22:45.680
Or you have four, what
seemingly looks like four,

00:22:45.680 --> 00:22:47.820
rows with one row here.

00:22:47.820 --> 00:22:50.870
So the first time I saw
that, I was like, OK.

00:22:50.870 --> 00:22:52.370
Let's see how far
we can take this.

00:22:52.370 --> 00:22:54.427
All right, well, do I need
to necessarily specify

00:22:54.427 --> 00:22:56.510
the string that you saw
in brackets there earlier?

00:22:56.510 --> 00:22:57.110
No, I don't.

00:22:57.110 --> 00:22:58.550
BigQuery smart enough
to recognize that,

00:22:58.550 --> 00:22:59.870
hey, if you're
passing in things that

00:22:59.870 --> 00:23:01.430
look like they're
in string notation,

00:23:01.430 --> 00:23:03.410
sure, I'll interpret
it as a string for you.

00:23:03.410 --> 00:23:06.301
As you see here, it's
still going to be a string.

00:23:06.301 --> 00:23:08.050
All right, well, what
are some cool things

00:23:08.050 --> 00:23:10.330
that can do once you have
your data inside of arrays

00:23:10.330 --> 00:23:13.240
or you're accessing a data
set that already has arrays?

00:23:13.240 --> 00:23:15.796
And by the way, the reason why
we're creating these arrays,

00:23:15.796 --> 00:23:17.170
normally, if you
had a field, you

00:23:17.170 --> 00:23:19.190
could just have the
field name in here.

00:23:19.190 --> 00:23:21.315
But we just wanted to show
you the special sauce is

00:23:21.315 --> 00:23:22.280
what's behind it.

00:23:22.280 --> 00:23:24.904
So we're setting
up our data set.

00:23:24.904 --> 00:23:26.320
And our data set
is just literally

00:23:26.320 --> 00:23:28.050
going to be the array
that you see here.

00:23:28.050 --> 00:23:31.450
It says give me this
array, an array of fruit.

00:23:31.450 --> 00:23:34.949
And with that fruit array,
I'm going to select something,

00:23:34.949 --> 00:23:36.490
I'm going to go into
the fruit array,

00:23:36.490 --> 00:23:42.264
and I'm going or pull back the
element in the second position.

00:23:42.264 --> 00:23:43.430
Anyone want to take a guess?

00:23:43.430 --> 00:23:45.846
Is it going to be a raspberry,
a blackberry, a strawberry,

00:23:45.846 --> 00:23:47.660
or a cherry at
position number two?

00:23:49.869 --> 00:23:52.160
Everyone says-- and when I
get to this huge conference,

00:23:52.160 --> 00:23:55.460
too, I'm like, uh-uh, berry.

00:23:55.460 --> 00:23:56.990
Strawberry is exactly correct.

00:23:56.990 --> 00:23:58.527
So if you worked
with arrays before,

00:23:58.527 --> 00:24:00.860
you can get easily tripped
up with like, all right, well

00:24:00.860 --> 00:24:03.170
is it zero-based
or is it one-based?

00:24:03.170 --> 00:24:04.280
There's one CC.

00:24:04.280 --> 00:24:07.699
Zero for raspberry,
blackberry, and then--

00:24:07.699 --> 00:24:09.740
blackberry's number one,
strawberry's number two.

00:24:09.740 --> 00:24:11.400
That's because
you're using offset.

00:24:11.400 --> 00:24:13.869
All right, if my aliases
don't give it away.

00:24:13.869 --> 00:24:15.410
People in the audience
are just like,

00:24:15.410 --> 00:24:16.940
I'm just reading his queries.

00:24:16.940 --> 00:24:19.277
You see that you can have
it as an ordinal, which

00:24:19.277 --> 00:24:21.110
is the same thing as
offset, except you have

00:24:21.110 --> 00:24:22.250
ordinals starting with one.

00:24:22.250 --> 00:24:25.100
So one, two is now
blackberry instead.

00:24:25.100 --> 00:24:26.710
So accessing array
elements, this

00:24:26.710 --> 00:24:28.310
is like a fundamental part
of like, OK, you've got

00:24:28.310 --> 00:24:29.790
your data into an array format.

00:24:29.790 --> 00:24:31.354
How do you actually
get it out of it?

00:24:31.354 --> 00:24:33.020
All right, what happens
if you did this?

00:24:33.020 --> 00:24:36.700
I've only got four
records in there.

00:24:36.700 --> 00:24:38.700
Well, it's going to look
for the 999th in there.

00:24:38.700 --> 00:24:39.574
Oh, I didn't find it.

00:24:39.574 --> 00:24:40.900
It's out of bounds, overflow.

00:24:40.900 --> 00:24:41.660
The good thing is,
in BigQuery, you're

00:24:41.660 --> 00:24:43.880
not charged for queries
that result in an error.

00:24:43.880 --> 00:24:46.230
And this is super friendly.

00:24:46.230 --> 00:24:49.100
I mean, I go to war with the
validator on a daily basis.

00:24:49.100 --> 00:24:50.600
But honestly, it's
your best friend,

00:24:50.600 --> 00:24:51.730
because you can
actually toggle it open,

00:24:51.730 --> 00:24:54.620
and you can see whether or not
your query is going to execute

00:24:54.620 --> 00:24:55.710
with an error or without.

00:24:55.710 --> 00:24:57.590
So if you get that
green checkmark,

00:24:57.590 --> 00:24:58.772
you're in good shape.

00:24:58.772 --> 00:25:00.480
What other things can
you do with arrays?

00:25:00.480 --> 00:25:02.430
You can do really cool
array aggregations.

00:25:02.430 --> 00:25:04.805
So if you want to see how many
elements are in the array,

00:25:04.805 --> 00:25:07.590
it's just a simple
function, array length.

00:25:07.590 --> 00:25:09.340
And your mind will
begin to think of like,

00:25:09.340 --> 00:25:10.464
oh, what do I want to know?

00:25:10.464 --> 00:25:12.741
Why does array length matter?

00:25:12.741 --> 00:25:14.490
For Carl's example
that he showed earlier,

00:25:14.490 --> 00:25:17.070
how many articles were
published in a day, which

00:25:17.070 --> 00:25:18.370
is the length of the array?

00:25:18.370 --> 00:25:19.780
Do mathematical
operations-- what

00:25:19.780 --> 00:25:27.810
if I wanted to do a little
bit more complex topics?

00:25:27.810 --> 00:25:29.600
Here's a big one.

00:25:29.600 --> 00:25:31.040
Exactly as Carl
mentioned before,

00:25:31.040 --> 00:25:33.290
this is the first point where
I hit my first stumbling

00:25:33.290 --> 00:25:35.210
block in trying to
really understand this.

00:25:35.210 --> 00:25:37.250
I have a field that's in array.

00:25:37.250 --> 00:25:39.110
I'm just hard coding
this array right here--

00:25:39.110 --> 00:25:40.730
apple, pear, and plum.

00:25:40.730 --> 00:25:42.910
And then I have a field
that's not in array.

00:25:42.910 --> 00:25:44.910
So this is where it
gets really interesting.

00:25:44.910 --> 00:25:46.760
So we've got repeated
string, and then we've

00:25:46.760 --> 00:25:48.789
got all of these items
in this array associated

00:25:48.789 --> 00:25:49.580
with this customer.

00:25:49.580 --> 00:25:52.280
But keep in mind, this is the
massive kind of learning point,

00:25:52.280 --> 00:25:54.890
is that this is on a
singular row still.

00:25:54.890 --> 00:25:58.880
So this is what's called
an unflattened array.

00:25:58.880 --> 00:26:00.970
Let's see what happens
when we flatten it.

00:26:00.970 --> 00:26:04.430
So in order to perform general
SQL operations on this,

00:26:04.430 --> 00:26:09.332
we need to get things into a
one element per row context.

00:26:09.332 --> 00:26:10.790
And this is where
the syntax starts

00:26:10.790 --> 00:26:14.550
to get the better of some
people, but it's not too bad.

00:26:14.550 --> 00:26:17.730
So let's see what we got.

00:26:17.730 --> 00:26:20.810
Now we're bringing back the
items, the customer names.

00:26:20.810 --> 00:26:24.830
And this is the magical
command, is unnest.

00:26:24.830 --> 00:26:26.090
It flattens everything out.

00:26:26.090 --> 00:26:28.970
So while you had one row
with three items before,

00:26:28.970 --> 00:26:32.180
we have essentially squished
it with one row per each

00:26:32.180 --> 00:26:33.790
of the items in the array.

00:26:33.790 --> 00:26:36.410
And you're doing what's called
a correlated cross join, which

00:26:36.410 --> 00:26:38.750
basically says, hey, remember
when I had apple, pear,

00:26:38.750 --> 00:26:40.520
and peach in just one Jacob?

00:26:40.520 --> 00:26:42.917
Just associate with that with
each of the items in there.

00:26:42.917 --> 00:26:45.500
It's not like a-- it is kind of
like a traditional cross join.

00:26:45.500 --> 00:26:47.510
But it's not cross
joining across all

00:26:47.510 --> 00:26:49.730
of the different
possible array values.

00:26:49.730 --> 00:26:51.710
It's just cross joining
against just that one

00:26:51.710 --> 00:26:53.430
that it's associated with.

00:26:53.430 --> 00:26:55.250
And this is actually
optional syntax.

00:26:55.250 --> 00:26:57.279
So if you ever see this
in code, that's fine.

00:26:57.279 --> 00:26:58.820
Also, you can just
have a comma there

00:26:58.820 --> 00:27:01.250
and that works, because that's
treated as a cross join.

00:27:01.250 --> 00:27:03.530
So unnest is the critical part.

00:27:03.530 --> 00:27:04.640
And the reason why--

00:27:04.640 --> 00:27:07.130
here I like to think of
unnest as, all right, well,

00:27:07.130 --> 00:27:08.960
unnest essentially
breaks apart my array

00:27:08.960 --> 00:27:11.900
into one element on
each separate row.

00:27:11.900 --> 00:27:13.520
But even more
philosophically, you

00:27:13.520 --> 00:27:15.800
can think of this
separate array that you're

00:27:15.800 --> 00:27:18.350
bringing in almost as
like a separate table.

00:27:18.350 --> 00:27:21.060
And that's why you're unnesting
this after the from statement.

00:27:21.060 --> 00:27:23.030
So typically in
SQL you say, select

00:27:23.030 --> 00:27:24.660
a lot of stuff from
a different table,

00:27:24.660 --> 00:27:26.480
join it on another table.

00:27:26.480 --> 00:27:30.050
That's kind of the simple
walkthrough for any data

00:27:30.050 --> 00:27:30.680
analyst.

00:27:30.680 --> 00:27:32.540
You're replacing
that by saying, I

00:27:32.540 --> 00:27:34.670
have all of my data
in this one table

00:27:34.670 --> 00:27:36.110
but at different hierarchies.

00:27:36.110 --> 00:27:37.717
How do I get it on
the same hierarchy?

00:27:37.717 --> 00:27:39.800
Well, instead have doing
the joins between tables,

00:27:39.800 --> 00:27:43.382
you're now unnesting
within that singular table.

00:27:43.382 --> 00:27:45.590
So other cool things you
can do,Z if you can imagine,

00:27:45.590 --> 00:27:49.160
you can find out what the order
of the element in the array

00:27:49.160 --> 00:27:50.540
was, super useful.

00:27:50.540 --> 00:27:54.971
And that's just simply buying
the reserve keyword offset

00:27:54.971 --> 00:27:56.470
And then here we
just have the index

00:27:56.470 --> 00:27:58.060
of each different
items in there,

00:27:58.060 --> 00:28:00.690
so you could find and do things
like minimums, maximums, latest

00:28:00.690 --> 00:28:02.106
item that's in there as well.

00:28:02.106 --> 00:28:03.730
So this is just the
tip of the iceberg.

00:28:03.730 --> 00:28:05.510
Carl and I have
limited time to present

00:28:05.510 --> 00:28:06.970
all of the cool
things you can do

00:28:06.970 --> 00:28:08.303
with nested and repeated fields.

00:28:08.303 --> 00:28:10.300
But there's two key takeaways.

00:28:10.300 --> 00:28:14.470
It's getting your data into
arrays is rather simple.

00:28:14.470 --> 00:28:17.230
And working with the
syntax, once it's in arrays,

00:28:17.230 --> 00:28:19.280
you're largely going
be working with unnest.

00:28:19.280 --> 00:28:21.527
So when you see unnest
in people's queries,

00:28:21.527 --> 00:28:23.110
all that means is
it's taking elements

00:28:23.110 --> 00:28:25.120
in an array that's
in a singular row,

00:28:25.120 --> 00:28:27.520
like five items in one
row, and having five items

00:28:27.520 --> 00:28:29.380
be in five separate rows.

00:28:29.380 --> 00:28:36.350
So we can do array creation.

00:28:36.350 --> 00:28:38.730
This is saying, I have a data
set that just has strings.

00:28:38.730 --> 00:28:39.979
We're going the other way now.

00:28:39.979 --> 00:28:42.434
So I have one, two, and
a third row for banana.

00:28:42.434 --> 00:28:43.600
Now we're going the reverse.

00:28:43.600 --> 00:28:46.180
We're packing all
those into an array.

00:28:46.180 --> 00:28:47.800
And you can't do
arrays of arrays.

00:28:47.800 --> 00:28:48.940
I'm going to show
you something just

00:28:48.940 --> 00:28:51.384
in a minute that has a really
cool container aspect of it.

00:28:51.384 --> 00:28:53.800
Same queries before, but if
you wanted to do a nice order,

00:28:53.800 --> 00:28:56.140
let's see if we can't
get apple, banana, pear.

00:29:02.470 --> 00:29:03.840
And you can-- there you go.

00:29:03.840 --> 00:29:05.284
Honestly, it's
much like you would

00:29:05.284 --> 00:29:07.700
treat another different table
in there as well, same order

00:29:07.700 --> 00:29:08.579
by works.

00:29:08.579 --> 00:29:10.120
All right, a little
bit more complex.

00:29:10.120 --> 00:29:11.995
What happens if I wanted
to do just a filter?

00:29:11.995 --> 00:29:14.140
I want a filter only
to include items

00:29:14.140 --> 00:29:16.427
that I want from one of
those particular rows.

00:29:16.427 --> 00:29:18.260
And here's where it
gets really interesting.

00:29:18.260 --> 00:29:20.360
So if you had
something like this,

00:29:20.360 --> 00:29:24.610
which is what we're going
to repeat in the next query,

00:29:24.610 --> 00:29:28.000
to get at, say I wanted to
return just the customers that

00:29:28.000 --> 00:29:31.420
had apples in
their fruit basket,

00:29:31.420 --> 00:29:33.227
I first need to
access that array.

00:29:33.227 --> 00:29:35.560
Remember, accessing the arrays
involves flattening them,

00:29:35.560 --> 00:29:37.018
squishing them into
different-- one

00:29:37.018 --> 00:29:39.050
row per each different
element in the array.

00:29:39.050 --> 00:29:40.216
So we need to first do that.

00:29:40.216 --> 00:29:43.600
So at the first, you'll
see, all right, well,

00:29:43.600 --> 00:29:45.790
this is our global list.

00:29:45.790 --> 00:29:49.085
Let's unnest that, flatten that.

00:29:49.085 --> 00:29:51.210
And then we can just operate
over just like normal,

00:29:51.210 --> 00:29:52.500
because row one is apple.

00:29:52.500 --> 00:29:53.250
Row two is banana.

00:29:53.250 --> 00:29:55.500
Row three is pear.

00:29:55.500 --> 00:29:57.900
We can just filter that just
like a normal SQL query.

00:29:57.900 --> 00:29:59.880
And then we can combine
it back into an array

00:29:59.880 --> 00:30:02.060
format at the outset.

00:30:02.060 --> 00:30:03.390
So it's going into the array.

00:30:03.390 --> 00:30:06.770
It's squishing it so
we can operate over it.

00:30:06.770 --> 00:30:09.930
And then it's putting it all
back together in an array.

00:30:09.930 --> 00:30:11.680
And you can see in the
query results here,

00:30:11.680 --> 00:30:14.110
this returns all
of the customers

00:30:14.110 --> 00:30:16.540
in this particular case,
the customer's baskets that

00:30:16.540 --> 00:30:18.590
had a apple in it.

00:30:18.590 --> 00:30:22.850
Notice that the third
row, or the third array,

00:30:22.850 --> 00:30:24.802
returns a null or an empty set.

00:30:24.802 --> 00:30:27.010
So keep that in mind when
you're working with arrays,

00:30:27.010 --> 00:30:29.200
is that, if the
condition doesn't match,

00:30:29.200 --> 00:30:31.316
in your traditional
joins, you might not

00:30:31.316 --> 00:30:33.440
get a record returned, but
you'll get an empty set,

00:30:33.440 --> 00:30:35.580
a null set.

00:30:35.580 --> 00:30:39.180
OK, so that is arrays.

00:30:39.180 --> 00:30:40.850
That's half of what
I call the magic.

00:30:40.850 --> 00:30:42.599
So if you understand
arrays and unnesting,

00:30:42.599 --> 00:30:44.900
that's 90% of the hard leg work.

00:30:44.900 --> 00:30:48.310
And honestly, it took
me a decent couple

00:30:48.310 --> 00:30:50.810
of weeks to really wrap my head
around the unnesting syntax.

00:30:50.810 --> 00:30:53.630
But philosophically, think
about it as a separate table.

00:30:53.630 --> 00:30:56.180
Now, Carl also mentioned
a really, really cool

00:30:56.180 --> 00:30:58.334
other native SQL
2011 syntax data

00:30:58.334 --> 00:30:59.750
type that's supported
by BigQuery,

00:30:59.750 --> 00:31:01.208
and that's the
struct or structure.

00:31:01.208 --> 00:31:02.537
Think of it like a container.

00:31:02.537 --> 00:31:04.370
So this is where it
gets really interesting.

00:31:04.370 --> 00:31:07.160
You have one field.

00:31:07.160 --> 00:31:10.910
We're specifying that
it can, unlike an array,

00:31:10.910 --> 00:31:12.827
contain different data
types within one field.

00:31:12.827 --> 00:31:15.243
So we're going to say it's an
integer for the first field,

00:31:15.243 --> 00:31:16.970
and it's a string
for the second field.

00:31:16.970 --> 00:31:19.830
And we're saying
35 for this field.

00:31:19.830 --> 00:31:21.380
We're not giving it a name yet.

00:31:21.380 --> 00:31:24.870
And we're saying a string
of Jacob for the second one.

00:31:24.870 --> 00:31:26.710
Any thoughts with
what's missing here?

00:31:26.710 --> 00:31:27.560
What looks funny about this?

00:31:27.560 --> 00:31:29.060
There's two things
that are missing.

00:31:29.060 --> 00:31:31.260
Any thoughts?

00:31:31.260 --> 00:31:32.630
Yeah, the names.

00:31:32.630 --> 00:31:37.790
So we're missing not only
the name of the field--

00:31:37.790 --> 00:31:38.670
let's execute this.

00:31:43.035 --> 00:31:45.370
If I just ran that piece--

00:31:45.370 --> 00:31:49.620
OK, so I'm specifying this
as 35 is going to be the age.

00:31:49.620 --> 00:31:50.890
Jacob is going to be the name.

00:31:50.890 --> 00:31:52.585
But also, since this
is a collection,

00:31:52.585 --> 00:31:55.060
right, it's a collection
of these fields,

00:31:55.060 --> 00:31:57.610
you need to give the
overall container a name.

00:31:57.610 --> 00:32:00.600
And this is the foundation of
the nested and repeated fields,

00:32:00.600 --> 00:32:02.590
where now I'm storing
something where

00:32:02.590 --> 00:32:04.840
I have multiple
fields associated

00:32:04.840 --> 00:32:06.790
with a single piece
of information,

00:32:06.790 --> 00:32:09.040
in this particular case,
customers, where I'm

00:32:09.040 --> 00:32:10.510
storing multiple data points.

00:32:10.510 --> 00:32:11.330
Why is this useful?

00:32:11.330 --> 00:32:13.829
If you're thinking in the back
of your head, that looks just

00:32:13.829 --> 00:32:17.230
like a table that I'm bringing
in, customers that age,

00:32:17.230 --> 00:32:20.020
customers that name, imagine
having other structs in here,

00:32:20.020 --> 00:32:23.140
storing things like items that
are in their shopping carts

00:32:23.140 --> 00:32:24.460
or transaction history.

00:32:24.460 --> 00:32:26.295
Instead of having
a massive schema

00:32:26.295 --> 00:32:27.670
with 15 different
things like you

00:32:27.670 --> 00:32:29.620
do in the transactional
world, you bring that all

00:32:29.620 --> 00:32:31.360
together into one
massive reporting table

00:32:31.360 --> 00:32:33.410
through the use of structs.

00:32:33.410 --> 00:32:35.350
Now, here is where it
gets really interesting.

00:32:35.350 --> 00:32:37.630
Of course, you are already
familiar with arrays.

00:32:37.630 --> 00:32:41.390
You can have arrays inside
of these containers as well.

00:32:41.390 --> 00:32:45.280
So now we're slowly building
together our grocery example

00:32:45.280 --> 00:32:45.880
here.

00:32:45.880 --> 00:32:48.730
We now have what you saw before,
the age and then the name.

00:32:48.730 --> 00:32:50.590
And then we're also
hard coding an array

00:32:50.590 --> 00:32:51.754
to get data type in there.

00:32:51.754 --> 00:32:53.420
So you saw this before,
repeated string.

00:32:53.420 --> 00:32:55.300
But now it's part
of a collection

00:32:55.300 --> 00:32:56.750
of customers in there as well.

00:32:59.280 --> 00:33:01.380
Now, I can go the opposite
direction as well.

00:33:01.380 --> 00:33:04.960
You can actually have an
array of struct data types.

00:33:08.140 --> 00:33:10.650
So the combination of
using arrays and structs

00:33:10.650 --> 00:33:11.850
is super powerful.

00:33:11.850 --> 00:33:14.430
The two key points that
I'll leave you with

00:33:14.430 --> 00:33:20.560
are, one, if you see a schema
that looks like this-- here,

00:33:20.560 --> 00:33:21.310
let me pull it up.

00:33:25.800 --> 00:33:28.170
As Carl has showed before--

00:33:28.170 --> 00:33:29.920
actually, I'll just
jump into Hacker News.

00:33:35.310 --> 00:33:36.780
Let me pull this up.

00:33:42.180 --> 00:33:44.060
This is the Google
Analytics schema.

00:33:44.060 --> 00:33:47.796
So Google dogfoods its own
schemas in the sense of,

00:33:47.796 --> 00:33:49.670
if we're telling you
guys this is a great way

00:33:49.670 --> 00:33:52.760
to store highly performant
data, you're going

00:33:52.760 --> 00:33:53.940
to see that out in the wild.

00:33:53.940 --> 00:33:55.640
So if you have Google
Analytics data,

00:33:55.640 --> 00:33:57.410
exactly as Carl was
showing you before,

00:33:57.410 --> 00:33:58.580
if you're looking
through your schema

00:33:58.580 --> 00:34:00.746
and you say, OK, integer,
integer, integer, integer,

00:34:00.746 --> 00:34:03.440
whoa, what is this record
here, that is a struct.

00:34:03.440 --> 00:34:04.490
It's a collection.

00:34:04.490 --> 00:34:06.140
And you see it's a
totals collection.

00:34:06.140 --> 00:34:08.870
So much like where you might
have had a separate table

00:34:08.870 --> 00:34:11.510
for session details about
the visitor and then session

00:34:11.510 --> 00:34:14.090
details about the
totals for the visitor,

00:34:14.090 --> 00:34:16.080
this is all now within
this same table.

00:34:16.080 --> 00:34:19.070
And the easiest way you can
find that is look for data types

00:34:19.070 --> 00:34:20.239
that have records.

00:34:20.239 --> 00:34:22.770
And I'll show you,
just as a fun example,

00:34:22.770 --> 00:34:24.556
you can have pretty
wide schemas now.

00:34:24.556 --> 00:34:26.139
So I don't know if
you can see others.

00:34:26.139 --> 00:34:29.270
There's 32 different
structs that

00:34:29.270 --> 00:34:32.550
are available within even just
a Google Analytics schema.

00:34:32.550 --> 00:34:36.489
And where it gets
really interesting,

00:34:36.489 --> 00:34:39.670
if you take this
even further, you

00:34:39.670 --> 00:34:41.906
can have structs
inside of structs.

00:34:41.906 --> 00:34:43.489
So for Google
Analytics, how it stores

00:34:43.489 --> 00:34:46.060
the data is global information
about the hits data at that one

00:34:46.060 --> 00:34:46.420
level.

00:34:46.420 --> 00:34:47.753
But what about particular pages?

00:34:47.753 --> 00:34:48.460
OK, sure.

00:34:48.460 --> 00:34:49.929
Hits that page,
that page passed.

00:34:49.929 --> 00:34:52.210
You can go almost like
Inception, a couple of layers

00:34:52.210 --> 00:34:53.360
deep there as well.

00:34:53.360 --> 00:34:55.810
So again, this is just kind
of scratching the surface

00:34:55.810 --> 00:34:57.950
with what we can do
with the time allotted.

00:34:57.950 --> 00:34:59.800
But it's a super
fun concept, even

00:34:59.800 --> 00:35:01.600
if you have your
existing data, consider

00:35:01.600 --> 00:35:03.610
working with getting
it into arrays,

00:35:03.610 --> 00:35:05.707
instructs, to see if there's
a performance impact.

00:35:05.707 --> 00:35:08.290
If you're doing a lot of joins
between your transactional data

00:35:08.290 --> 00:35:10.240
to get it in one
place, perhaps store it

00:35:10.240 --> 00:35:13.300
on the reporting data table
side into a nested and repeated

00:35:13.300 --> 00:35:13.920
format.

00:35:13.920 --> 00:35:16.128
And see, instead of doing
those potentially expensive

00:35:16.128 --> 00:35:19.210
SQL joins on your
queries, if you

00:35:19.210 --> 00:35:21.970
can't get more performance
benefit from doing nest

00:35:21.970 --> 00:35:23.770
and repeated.

